  "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ f(-1) & =(-1+2)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(-1) & =-(-1+2)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(-1) & =2(-1+2)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-6(x+2)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(-1) & =-6(-1+2)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =1+(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+1)^{3}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1-(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}-(x+1)^{3}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
